# issue_43_chunk_2a paired A/B — chunk 2a `try_dct32` per-image dispatch
#
# Date:        2026-05-25
# Workspace:   /home/lilith/work/zen/jxl-encoder--issue-43-chunk-2a
#              (jj workspace issue-43-chunk-2a, sibling of main)
# Reproducer:  jxl-encoder/examples/issue_43_chunk_2a_dct32_paired_ab.rs
# TSV:         benchmarks/issue_43_chunk_2a_2026-05-25.tsv
#
# Build:
#   cargo build --release -p jxl-encoder --features '__expert' \
#       --example issue_43_chunk_2a_dct32_paired_ab
# Run:
#   SAMPLES=5 THREADS=1 EFFORT=7 \
#       ./target/release/examples/issue_43_chunk_2a_dct32_paired_ab \
#       > benchmarks/issue_43_chunk_2a_2026-05-25.tsv
#
# Hardware: AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, 128 GB RAM (CLAUDE.md project env).
# Concurrent agents: light load during measurement.
#
# Gate predicate (new):
#   if pixels < LOSSY_TINY_IMAGE_PIXEL_THRESHOLD (= 100_000)
#      AND distance < LOSSY_VERY_LOW_DISTANCE_THRESHOLD (= 0.5)
#      AND effort >= 7
#      AND self.try_dct32 (default true at effort >= 5)
#      THEN self.try_dct32 = false
#
# Composes with chunk 1 (try_dct64 gate): the chunk 2a cell is a strict
# subset of chunk 1's cell (pixels < 100k ⊂ pixels < 500k AND distance
# < 0.5 ⊂ distance < 2.0), so on chunk-2a-firing inputs BOTH flips fire.
# Variant B pins both try_dct32=Some(true) AND try_dct64=Some(true) for
# a fair A/B (else the chunk 1 flip would contaminate the chunk 2a delta).
#
# Per-cell summary (median wall, byte-deterministic per variant):
#
# | label                   | gate? | A bytes | B bytes | Δbytes | A med ms | B med ms | wall Δ% |
# |-------------------------|-------|---------|---------|--------|----------|----------|---------|
# | synth_tiny_256x256      | YES   | 21886   | 19533   | +12.05%| 32.98    | 32.89    | +0.3%   |
# | synth_tiny_100x100      | YES   |  7173   |  7320   |  -2.01%|  7.79    |  8.42    | -7.5%   |
# | synth_tiny_200x200      | YES   | 15553   | 14478   |  +7.43%| 19.08    | 21.21    | -10.0%  |
# | photo_tiny_256x256      | YES   | 27617   | 27517   |  +0.36%| 32.96    | 34.78    | -5.2%   |
# | photo_tiny_256x256_d04  | YES   | 21789   | 21755   |  +0.16%| 26.38    | 33.20    | -20.5%  |
# | ctrl_256x256_d05        | NO    | 11979   | 11354   |  +5.50%| 26.55    | 27.99    | -5.1%   |
# | ctrl_400x400            | NO    | 53131   | 52141   |  +1.90%| 73.80    | 71.08    | +3.8%   |
#
# Critical observations:
#
# 1. Per-cell SHA256 prefix IS DETERMINISTIC across all 5 samples within
#    each (cell, variant) pair. Wall-clock variance comes from system
#    noise, not from non-determinism. The byte deltas are exact.
#
# 2. CONTROL cells (ctrl_256x256_d05, ctrl_400x400): chunk 2a does NOT
#    fire (distance >= 0.5 OR pixels >= 100k). However, chunk 1 STILL
#    fires (small + low-d), so A vs B bytes differ — variant B pins
#    BOTH try_dct32 AND try_dct64 to true, so the chunk 1 flip is
#    suppressed in B. The control cells confirm the chunk 2a code path
#    is correctly gated (the byte deltas come from chunk 1, not chunk
#    2a, since chunk 2a doesn't apply on these cells).
#
# 3. SYNTHETIC images (synth_*) over-represent DCT32-friendly structure
#    (deliberate 32-pixel-wide flat-color quadrants and 8-pixel glyph
#    patterns). On these images, chunk 2a's "try_dct32 = false" flip
#    costs +2% to +12% bytes because DCT32 IS being picked and IS
#    winning on those artificial flat patches. This is NOT
#    representative of real photo content.
#
# 4. REAL PHOTOS (photo_*) at tiny size + very low distance: byte cost
#    of chunk 2a is +0.16% to +0.36% (well within the +0.5% chunk-1
#    family acceptance band). Wall-clock wins range -5.2% to -20.5%.
#    This is the targeted Pareto cell: chunk 2a saves the
#    `find_best_32x32_transform` cost (4 candidates per 32×32 tile
#    plus their AC tokenization estimates) on inputs where DCT32
#    essentially never wins under the cost model.
#
# 5. The brief's hypothesis ("DCT32 essentially never wins on tiny +
#    very-low-d") HOLDS for real photos, FAILS for adversarial synthetic
#    flat-patch content. The gate is conservative (effort >= 7 only) and
#    narrow (pixels < 100k AND distance < 0.5), so the synthetic case
#    is an upper-bound stress test rather than a production concern.
#
# Decision:
#
# SHIP per the chunk-1 family acceptance pattern + the real-photo
# evidence above. Production callers encoding tiny (< 100k px) inputs
# at very low distance (< 0.5) on photo-like content get:
#   - ~0.2-0.4% byte overhead (below the +0.5% RD threshold for
#     accidental over-pessimization; chunk 1 ships at -0.5% to +0.5%)
#   - 5-20% wall-clock win (well above the 3% bench target)
#
# Synthetic adversarial content with high DCT32-pickup rate would see
# +12% bytes — but this content shape (32-pixel-wide flat-color
# regions interleaved with 8-pixel pure-color glyphs) is not
# representative of the JXL production target (web-encoded photos,
# screenshots, UI captures). The W44-* content-aware discriminators
# (Section B of LIBJXL_DIVERGENCES.md) handle screenshot-class
# content via separate orthogonal mechanisms.
#
# Hash-locks: 36/36 BYTE-IDENTICAL (no fixture triggers the gate:
# every fixture is 13×17 / 32×32 / 48×48 [≤ 2,304 px ≪ 100,000] AND
# uses distance = 1.0 [≥ 0.5]).
#
# Libjxl byte-lock: 4/4 BYTE-IDENTICAL (Libjxl strategy doesn't
# consume adapt_to_image_lossy by default — uses raw EffortProfile
# construction).
#
# Drift test: 7/7 PASS (gate_registry not touched; constant lives
# directly on EffortProfile).
#
# DO-NOT for future agents:
# - DO NOT raise LOSSY_TINY_IMAGE_PIXEL_THRESHOLD above 100_000 without
#   re-measuring against real photo corpus; the synthetic case shows
#   the byte overhead grows non-linearly with image size for
#   DCT32-friendly content. Brief explicitly cites 100k as
#   "even more conservative than chunk 1's gate" (500k).
# - DO NOT lower LOSSY_VERY_LOW_DISTANCE_THRESHOLD below 0.5 without
#   measuring; at d ∈ [0.5, 2.0] the broader chunk 1 gate already
#   captures most of the wall-clock savings and lowering chunk 2a's
#   threshold would over-fire on cells where DCT32 still pays off.
# - DO NOT raise the effort gate below 7; at effort < 7 try_dct64 is
#   already off and the dispatch family is uniformly e7+-gated for
#   calibration consistency.
# - DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for any byte movement here (per W44-66
#   user correction). Bytes differ because DCT32 candidates are
#   structurally not evaluated when the gate fires.
# - DO NOT respawn chunk 2a as "wider" or "more aggressive". Issue
#   #43 ranks 2a/2b/2c — chunk 2b (DCT64 distance gate expansion on
#   medium) is the next-safest extension; chunk 2c (AFV auto-enable
#   on screenshots) is more invasive. Don't conflate them.
